From 59ed2d9673b6c4f066733777abae511c87a1e9af Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 14sea <wanhuaning@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 17 May 2026 16:04:27 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Phase 3 NH8 v3 (diag firmware): B5b/B5c steps exercising
 CTRL.b7
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Diagnostic-firmware extension to patch 0010's mode_epcs_probe.
Adds two post-DATAOUT-read steps that exercise the CTRL.b7
ctrl_rden_drop pulse introduced by patch 0013:

  B5b — write CTRL = READ | RDEN_DROP, snapshot STATUS
  B5c — bounded 10M-iter wait for busy=0 (with periodic STATUS
        print every ~256k iters); reports OK or TIMEOUT with the
        final STATUS value

Splits cleanly from the wrapper-only NH8 v3 (patch 0013).  Reason
for split: wrapper RTL change is a hardware-interface extension
useful for any future EPCS code path; the firmware steps are a
diagnostic instrumentation on top of patch 0010's STATUS-trace
probe specifically.  Logically two concerns, now two patches.

POST-HW TEST RESULT — same as patch 0013 (see its commit message
+ docs/captures/diag_nh8v3_rden_pulse_noop.log md5 b33a3230):
B5b shows STATUS=0x03 immediately after the rden_drop pulse
(no change from B6 baseline).  B5c bounded wait TIMEOUT with
final STATUS=0x03 (busy never deasserts after the firmware
pulse).  Confirms the single-cycle rden=0 window misses the
megafunction's stage_cntr=2'b10 window for end_read_reg to fire.

Stage2 ELF: 15332 → 15544 B (+212 B for the new diag prints +
bounded wait counter).

Patch retained: B5b/B5c are the empirical evidence record that
the firmware-pulse approach was tested and refuted.  Without
them, the trace would look identical to baseline diagnostic and
the refutation wouldn't be visible in the capture.  Future
session needs these to confirm whether megafunction regen
actually changes B5b/B5c behavior — until then, they show the
deadlock.

Companion patch: 0013 (NH8 v3 wrapper RTL — adds CTRL.b7 +
ctrl_rden_drop + am_rden).  This patch (0014) DEPENDS on 0013;
the CTRL_RDEN_DROP bit literal in main.c (1u << 7) only works
when wb_altasmi_parallel.v actually wires that bit through.
---
 sw/stage2_loader/main.c | 26 +++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/sw/stage2_loader/main.c b/sw/stage2_loader/main.c
index 2b0fd3e..8a0a05d 100644
--- a/sw/stage2_loader/main.c
+++ b/sw/stage2_loader/main.c
@@ -293,10 +293,11 @@ static void mode_epcs_probe(void)
     #define DP_DATAOUT ((volatile uint32_t *)(DP_BASE + 0x10))
     #define DP_ESTAT   ((volatile uint32_t *)(DP_BASE + 0x14))
 
-    /* CTRL bits (from epcs.c) */
+    /* CTRL bits (from epcs.c + NH8 v3 wrapper) */
     #define DC_READ        (1u << 0)
     #define DC_RD_STATUS   (1u << 3)
     #define DC_SHIFT_BYTES (1u << 4)
+    #define DC_RDEN_DROP   (1u << 7)   /* NH8 v3: drops rden for 1 cycle */
 
     /* STATUS bits (from epcs.c — wrapper concat {ill_w, ill_e, dv, busy}) */
     /* STATUS_BUSY=b0, STATUS_DV=b1, STATUS_ILL_ERASE=b2, STATUS_ILL_WRITE=b3 */
@@ -428,6 +429,29 @@ static void mode_epcs_probe(void)
         }
     }
 
+    /* NH8 v3: pulse rden_drop to terminate the read.  This is the last
+     * byte (we asked for 1), so drop rden for one cycle to fire
+     * end_read_reg → busy_wire deasserts → operation completes. */
+    *DP_CTRL = DC_READ | DC_RDEN_DROP;
+    uart_puts("[P] B5b after CTRL=READ|RDEN_DROP STATUS=");
+    uart_puthex32(*DP_STATUS);
+    uart_puts("\r\n");
+
+    /* Bounded wait for busy=0 after rden drop. */
+    {
+        uint32_t i;
+        uint32_t s = 0;
+        for (i = 0; i < 10000000u; i++) {
+            s = *DP_STATUS;
+            if (!(s & 0x1u)) break;
+        }
+        uart_puts("[P] B5c post_rden_drop_wait i=");
+        uart_puthex32(i);
+        uart_puts(" final=");
+        uart_puthex32(s);
+        uart_puts((i >= 10000000u) ? " TIMEOUT\r\n" : " OK\r\n");
+    }
+
     *DP_CTRL = 0;
     uart_puts("[P] B6 after CTRL=0 STATUS=");
     uart_puthex32(*DP_STATUS);
-- 
